meta {
  name: Create student with a new identity [outcome unverified]
  type: http
  seq: 18
}

post {
  url: {{baseUrl}}/api/v1/students
  body: json
  auth: inherit
}

headers {
  X-School-Id: {{schoolCode}}
}

body:json {
  {
    "person": {
      "username": "ishaan.verma",
      "email": "ishaan.verma@example.com",
      "first_name": "Ishaan",
      "last_name": "Verma",
      "display_name": "Ishaan Verma",
      "group_keys": ["students"]
    },
    "admission_number": "ADM-2026-0002",
    "admission_date": "2026-04-01"
  }
}

tests {
  // Same reasoning as request 14, which this shares its CAS call with: no
  // status assert, because none of us has seen the answer. The 201 arm is the
  // one that needs acting on, not just recording - it leaves two rows behind
  // that folder 12 does not delete.
  const explained = [201, 501, 502];
  const meaning = {
    201: "a student AND a person now exist. Save their ids, delete them by hand, and add the two deletes to folder 12 before anyone runs this again.",
    501: "provisioning refused before CAS was called. Nothing was created.",
    502: "CAS was called and answered badly. Nothing was created here, but check CAS for a half-made identity.",
    401: "you ran without a token, so this request says nothing about provisioning either way."
  };

  test("inline provisioning answers something we can account for", function () {
    const status = res.getStatus();
    console.log("POST /students with an inline person returned " + status + " - " +
      (meaning[status] || "an outcome none of us predicted; work out why before changing this test"));
    if (status === 201) {
      console.log("  student_id=" + res.body.id + " person_id=" + res.body.person_id + " - these are NOT cleaned up");
    }
    expect(explained).to.include(status);
  });
}

docs {
  UNVERIFIED. Read the tests block before you trust anything here.

  This is the interesting shape on this surface: send person_id for someone who
  already exists, or send a person object and the CAS identity is minted as
  part of creating the student. The same option exists on POST /staff and
  POST /guardians. One capability covers both paths - core.students.create here,
  core.staff.write and core.guardians.create for the other two - so whoever may
  admit a student may also, by that fact, cause a login to exist for them.

  The outcome is open for exactly the reason request 14's is: this goes through
  the same provisioner, so it answers 201, 502 or 501 under the same conditions,
  and 13 spells out which is which. What differs is the cost of being wrong.

  If this returns 201 it creates a student and a person that folder 12 knows
  nothing about - it deletes by the ids saved in requests 02 to 09, and these
  two are not among them. That gap is deliberate: a delete for rows that cannot
  currently exist is dead weight that looks like coverage. Whoever makes
  provisioning work owns adding the two deletes, and will find this note in the
  request that caused the problem.

  A re-run after a 201 is the second trap: admission_number is unique per
  school, so ADM-2026-0002 comes back 409 until the leftover row is gone.

  The inline object is only accepted when creating. PATCH with it and you get a
  400 - request 19.
}
